{"authored" [] "sources" [{"question" "q-125738" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the vector field $F(x,y)=⟨2x{y}^{2},\\,2y{x}^{2}⟩$ and the points $A(1,1)$ and $B(2,8)$ in the $xy$-plane. \n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- $F$ is conservative.\n- ${\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=255,$ where ${C}_{1}$ is the path along the line segment $\\overset{―}{AB},$ traversed from $A$ to $B.$\n- ${\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=255,$ where ${C}_{2}$ is the path along the curve $y={x}^{3},$ traversed from $A$ to $B.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069873/state.json" "file_sha256" "caa9be6dbd7b5e91452f30b7eeec8ffd7b404e03d59f1ac6c23569f8401f487b"} {"question" "q-125738" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Let $C$ be a piecewise-smooth curve given by the vector-valued function $r(t)$ for $t\\in [a,b].$ Let $f$ be a differentiable function of two or three variables such that $∇f$ is continuous on $C.$ The fundamental theorem for line integrals states that \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r=f(r(b))-f(r(a)).\n$$\n\n \n\nThis theorem implies that the line integral of a conservative vector field depends only on the value of $f$ at the initial and terminal points $A$ and $B,$ and is independent of the path between them.\n\nWith that in mind, we consider each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Notice that $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j$ is a vector field on ${R}^{2}$ with domain $D={R}^{2}.$ Computing the partial derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=4xy.\n$$\n\nSince the partial derivatives exist and are continuous and equal everywhere on ${R}^{2},$ we conclude that $F$ is conservative.\n\n- Statement II is true. Since $F$ is conservative, there exists a function $f$ such that $∇f=F.$ Reconstructing the function $f$ from its gradient in the usual way, we get\n\n$$\nf={x}^{2}{y}^{2}.\n$$\n\nApplying the fundamental theorem for line integrals, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r & ={\\int }_{{C}_{1}}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r \\\\  & =f(2,8)-f(1,1) \\\\  & ={2}^{2}\\cdot {8}^{2}-{1}^{2}\\cdot {1}^{2} \\\\  & =4\\cdot 64-1 \\\\  & =255.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. Since $F$ is conservative and ${C}_{2}$ has the same initial and terminal points as ${C}_{1},$ we have\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r={\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=255.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069873/state.json" "file_sha256" "caa9be6dbd7b5e91452f30b7eeec8ffd7b404e03d59f1ac6c23569f8401f487b"} {"question" "q-125738" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069873/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "b03f4ecb2735fc9ae1a7ae2623d52246da314669040bed54c8cceab19a4b426a"} {"question" "q-125738" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069873/state.json" "file_sha256" "caa9be6dbd7b5e91452f30b7eeec8ffd7b404e03d59f1ac6c23569f8401f487b"} {"question" "q-125738" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and II only"] ["text" "I and III only"] ["text" "I, II, and III"] ["text" "II only"] ["text" "III only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069873/state.json" "file_sha256" "caa9be6dbd7b5e91452f30b7eeec8ffd7b404e03d59f1ac6c23569f8401f487b"} {"question" "q-125738" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "I, II, and III"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069873/state.json" "file_sha256" "caa9be6dbd7b5e91452f30b7eeec8ffd7b404e03d59f1ac6c23569f8401f487b"} {"question" "q-125748" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the vector field $F(x,y)=2xy{e}^{{x}^{2}}\\,i+{e}^{{x}^{2}}\\,j.$ Calculate ${\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r,$ where $C$ is any smooth path from $(-1,1)$ to $(1,-1)$ in ${R}^{2}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069873/state.json" "file_sha256" "caa9be6dbd7b5e91452f30b7eeec8ffd7b404e03d59f1ac6c23569f8401f487b"} {"question" "q-125748" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Let $C$ be a piecewise-smooth curve given by the vector-valued function $r(t)$ for $t\\in [a,b].$ Let $f$ be a differentiable function of two or three variables such that $∇f$ is continuous on $C.$ The fundamental theorem for line integrals states that \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r=f(r(b))-f(r(a)).\n$$\n\n \n\nThis theorem implies that the line integral of a conservative vector field depends only on the value of $f$ at the initial and terminal points $A$ and $B,$ and is independent of the path between them.\n\nIn our case, the coordinates of the endpoints of the path $C$ are $A(-1,1)$ and $B(1,-1).$\n\nNotice that $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j$ is a vector field on ${R}^{2}$ with domain $D={R}^{2}.$ Computing the partial derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=2x{e}^{{x}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nSince the partial derivatives exist and are continuous and equal everywhere on ${R}^{2},$ we conclude that $F$ is conservative. Therefore, we know there exists a function $f$ such that $∇f=F.$\n\nReconstructing $f$ from its gradient in the usual way, we get\n\n$$\nf=y{e}^{{x}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nFinally, applying the fundamental theorem for line integrals, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r & ={\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r \\\\  & =f(1,-1)-f(-1,1) \\\\  & =(-1){e}^{{1}^{2}}-(1){e}^{(-1)^{2}} \\\\  & =-e-e \\\\  & =-2e.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069873/state.json" "file_sha256" "caa9be6dbd7b5e91452f30b7eeec8ffd7b404e03d59f1ac6c23569f8401f487b"} {"question" "q-125748" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069873/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "b03f4ecb2735fc9ae1a7ae2623d52246da314669040bed54c8cceab19a4b426a"} {"question" "q-125748" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069873/state.json" "file_sha256" "caa9be6dbd7b5e91452f30b7eeec8ffd7b404e03d59f1ac6c23569f8401f487b"} {"question" "q-125748" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-2e"] ["math" "-e"] ["math" "1-e"] ["math" "2e-1"] ["math" "e"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069873/state.json" "file_sha256" "caa9be6dbd7b5e91452f30b7eeec8ffd7b404e03d59f1ac6c23569f8401f487b"} {"question" "q-125748" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "-2e"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069873/state.json" "file_sha256" "caa9be6dbd7b5e91452f30b7eeec8ffd7b404e03d59f1ac6c23569f8401f487b"} {"question" "q-125749" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the vector field $F(x,y)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡y\\,i+x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y\\,j.$ Calculate ${\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r,$ where $C$ is any smooth path from $(-2,0)$ to $(1,\\frac{\\pi }{2})$ in ${R}^{2}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069873/state.json" "file_sha256" "caa9be6dbd7b5e91452f30b7eeec8ffd7b404e03d59f1ac6c23569f8401f487b"} {"question" "q-125749" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Let $C$ be a piecewise-smooth curve given by the vector-valued function $r(t)$ for $t\\in [a,b].$ Let $f$ be a differentiable function of two or three variables such that $∇f$ is continuous on $C.$ The fundamental theorem for line integrals states that \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r=f(r(b))-f(r(a)).\n$$\n\n \n\nThis theorem implies that the line integral of a conservative vector field depends only on the value of $f$ at the initial and terminal points $A$ and $B,$ and is independent of the path between them.\n\nIn our case, the coordinates of the endpoints of the path $C$ are $A(-2,0)$ and $B(1,\\frac{\\pi }{2}).$\n\nNotice that $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j$ is a vector field on ${R}^{2}$ with domain $D={R}^{2}.$ Computing the partial derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=\\operatorname{cos}⁡y.\n$$\n\nSince the partial derivatives exist and are continuous and equal everywhere on ${R}^{2},$ we conclude that $F$ is conservative. Therefore, we know there exists a function $f$ such that $∇f=F.$\n\nReconstructing $f$ from its gradient in the usual way, we get\n\n$$\nf=x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y.\n$$\n\nFinally, applying the fundamental theorem for line integrals, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r & ={\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r \\\\  & =f(1,\\frac{\\pi }{2})-f(-2,0) \\\\  & =1\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})-(-2)\\operatorname{sin}⁡0 \\\\  & =1-0 \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069873/state.json" "file_sha256" "caa9be6dbd7b5e91452f30b7eeec8ffd7b404e03d59f1ac6c23569f8401f487b"} {"question" "q-125749" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069873/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "b03f4ecb2735fc9ae1a7ae2623d52246da314669040bed54c8cceab19a4b426a"} {"question" "q-125749" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069873/state.json" "file_sha256" "caa9be6dbd7b5e91452f30b7eeec8ffd7b404e03d59f1ac6c23569f8401f487b"} {"question" "q-125749" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-1"] ["math" "-\\frac{1}{2}"] ["math" "1"] ["math" "2"] ["math" "\\frac{3}{2}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069873/state.json" "file_sha256" "caa9be6dbd7b5e91452f30b7eeec8ffd7b404e03d59f1ac6c23569f8401f487b"} {"question" "q-125749" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "1"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069873/state.json" "file_sha256" "caa9be6dbd7b5e91452f30b7eeec8ffd7b404e03d59f1ac6c23569f8401f487b"} {"question" "q-125753" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the vector field $F(x,y)=2x{e}^{y}\\,i+{x}^{2}{e}^{y}\\,j.$ Calculate ${\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r,$ where $C$ is any smooth path from $(-1,0)$ to $(2,1)$ in ${R}^{2}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069873/state.json" "file_sha256" "caa9be6dbd7b5e91452f30b7eeec8ffd7b404e03d59f1ac6c23569f8401f487b"} {"question" "q-125753" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Let $C$ be a piecewise-smooth curve given by the vector-valued function $r(t)$ for $t\\in [a,b].$ Let $f$ be a differentiable function of two or three variables such that $∇f$ is continuous on $C.$ The fundamental theorem for line integrals states that \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r=f(r(b))-f(r(a)).\n$$\n\n \n\nThis theorem implies that the line integral of a conservative vector field depends only on the value of $f$ at the initial and terminal points $A$ and $B,$ and is independent of the path between them.\n\nIn our case, the coordinates of the endpoints of the path $C$ are $A(-1,0)$ and $B(2,1).$\n\nNotice that $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j$ is a vector field on ${R}^{2}$ with domain $D={R}^{2}.$ Computing the partial derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=2x{e}^{y}.\n$$\n\nSince the partial derivatives exist and are continuous and equal everywhere on ${R}^{2},$ we conclude that $F$ is conservative. Therefore, we know there exists a function $f$ such that $∇f=F.$\n\nReconstructing $f$ from its gradient in the usual way, we get\n\n$$\nf={x}^{2}{e}^{y}.\n$$\n\nFinally, applying the fundamental theorem for line integrals, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r & ={\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r \\\\  & =f(2,1)-f(-1,0) \\\\  & =({2}^{2}){e}^{1}-(-1)^{2}{e}^{0} \\\\  & =4e-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069873/state.json" "file_sha256" "caa9be6dbd7b5e91452f30b7eeec8ffd7b404e03d59f1ac6c23569f8401f487b"} {"question" "q-125753" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069873/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "b03f4ecb2735fc9ae1a7ae2623d52246da314669040bed54c8cceab19a4b426a"} {"question" "q-125753" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069873/state.json" "file_sha256" "caa9be6dbd7b5e91452f30b7eeec8ffd7b404e03d59f1ac6c23569f8401f487b"} {"question" "q-125753" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "1-e"] ["math" "2{e}^{2}-3"] ["math" "3{e}^{2}"] ["math" "4e-1"] ["math" "5e-2"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069873/state.json" "file_sha256" "caa9be6dbd7b5e91452f30b7eeec8ffd7b404e03d59f1ac6c23569f8401f487b"} {"question" "q-125753" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "4e-1"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069873/state.json" "file_sha256" "caa9be6dbd7b5e91452f30b7eeec8ffd7b404e03d59f1ac6c23569f8401f487b"} {"question" "q-125793" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the vector field $F(x,y)=⟨{y}^{2},\\,-x⟩$ and the points $O(0,0)$ and $A(4,2)$ in the $xy$-plane. \n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- ${\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=\\frac{5}{6},$ where ${C}_{1}$ is the path along the line segment $\\overset{―}{OA},$ traversed from $O$ to $A.$\n- ${\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=\\frac{16}{3},$ where ${C}_{2}$ is the path along the curve $y=\\sqrt{x},$ traversed from $O$ to $A.$\n- ${\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r$ is independent of path.\n- $F$ is conservative." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069873/state.json" "file_sha256" "caa9be6dbd7b5e91452f30b7eeec8ffd7b404e03d59f1ac6c23569f8401f487b"} {"question" "q-125793" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Let $C$ be a piecewise-smooth curve given by the vector-valued function $r(t)$ for $t\\in [a,b].$ Let $f$ be a differentiable function of two or three variables such that $∇f$ is continuous on $C.$ The fundamental theorem for line integrals states that \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r=f(r(b))-f(r(a)).\n$$\n\n \n\nThis theorem implies that the line integral of a conservative vector field depends only on the value of $f$ at the initial and terminal points $A$ and $B,$ and is independent of the path between them.\n\nWith that in mind, we consider each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. Parameterizing the line segment, we have\n\n$$\n{r}_{1}(t)=⟨4t,\\,2t⟩,\\,t\\in [0,1].\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r & ={\\int }_{a}^{b}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}⟨4{t}^{2},\\,-4t⟩\\cdot ⟨4,\\,2⟩\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}16{t}^{2}-8t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =8\\,{\\int }_{0}^{1}2{t}^{2}-t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =8{[\\frac{2{t}^{3}}{3}-\\frac{{t}^{2}}{2}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =8[\\frac{2}{3}-\\frac{1}{2}-0] \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. Parameterizing the curve, we have\n\n$$\n{r}_{2}(t)=⟨{t}^{2},\\,t⟩,\\,t\\in [0,2].\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r & ={\\int }_{a}^{b}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}⟨{t}^{2},\\,-{t}^{2}⟩\\cdot ⟨2t,\\,1⟩\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}2{t}^{3}-{t}^{2}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\frac{{t}^{4}}{2}-\\frac{{t}^{3}}{3}|}_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =(\\frac{{2}^{4}}{2}-\\frac{{2}^{3}}{3})-0 \\\\  & =8-\\frac{8}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{16}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since ${C}_{1}$ and ${C}_{2}$ have the same initial and terminal points, yet\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r\\ne {\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r,\n$$\n\nwe conclude that ${\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r$ is path-dependent.\n\n- Statement IV is false. If a vector field is conservative then it is path-independent. Since $F$ is path-dependent, it is not conservative." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069873/state.json" "file_sha256" "caa9be6dbd7b5e91452f30b7eeec8ffd7b404e03d59f1ac6c23569f8401f487b"} {"question" "q-125793" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069873/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "b03f4ecb2735fc9ae1a7ae2623d52246da314669040bed54c8cceab19a4b426a"} {"question" "q-125793" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069873/state.json" "file_sha256" "caa9be6dbd7b5e91452f30b7eeec8ffd7b404e03d59f1ac6c23569f8401f487b"} {"question" "q-125793" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and II only"] ["text" "I and III only"] ["text" "I only"] ["text" "II only"] ["text" "II, III, and IV only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069873/state.json" "file_sha256" "caa9be6dbd7b5e91452f30b7eeec8ffd7b404e03d59f1ac6c23569f8401f487b"} {"question" "q-125793" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "II only"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069873/state.json" "file_sha256" "caa9be6dbd7b5e91452f30b7